   <dl class='publication-summary'> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Problem</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>Regularization-based continual learning reduces catastrophic
forgetting by penalizing changes to parameters that mattered for earlier tasks.
Elastic Weight Consolidation (EWC), a standard instance, weights its quadratic
penalty by the empirical Fisher, usually justified as approximating loss
curvature.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Idea</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>We instead derive the penalty from what training on a new task
should preserve. In this formulation, <span class='pplri7t-x-x-109'>Continual Learning as an Optimization
Constraint </span>(CLOC), new training must keep chosen functionals of learned
behavior unchanged, and the local approximation of this constraint is the stored
penalty.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Insight</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>We show that EWC’s empirical Fisher is exactly the first-order
form of this constraint when the preserved functional is each old sample’s loss.
The derivation exposes a flaw: EWC weights each sample by the scale of its loss
gradient, so samples the model fits well barely contribute, although later
training can still forget them.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Method</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>Improved EWC (IEWC) instead
penalizes how far each old output moves off its loss level set, rather
than how much its loss changes, which removes this weighting for any
differentiable loss and output geometry.</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Evidence</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>Across classification,
segmentation, language, forecasting, and diffusion, IEWC variants match
or improve on EWC and outperform EWC-DR, a classification-specific
correction. In a held-out evaluation with validation-selected hyperparameters,
removing this weighting alone raises ImageNet-R accuracy over EWC by
2.00 percentage points (95% CI 0.93–3.07).</dd></div> <div class='publication-summary-part'><dt class='publication-summary-label'>Significance</dt><dd class='publication-summary-text'>More broadly,
CLOC turns the design of continual-learning penalties into a choice of
what to preserve, from which a principled penalty follows by derivation.</dd></div>
</dl>
